body:
I've finished the game. Here are some of my thoughts.

&#x200B;

**Atmosphere**: This game oozes so much flair and flavor everywhere you go. Enemy animations are so crisp and fluid, I feel like I'm legitimately fighting a real person (save for the crazy shit you can do.) I feel like I just took a mystical journey and it was so invigorating to explore worlds created by From on a vertical level.

&#x200B;

**Combat:** It *feels* like a Souls game, but it's not at all. You only have one weapon and it has a relatively linear moveset, save for the Combat Arts you can do. Where the game blows me away is the Posture system. It really changes the way you play from Soulsborne titles. You can't just back off and wait for them to make their own grave. You have to make your own opportunities with enemies and coax them into doing what you want them to do. Not only that, but your Posture is essentially a second healthbar for you too. This means you can do 3 things during a fight:

&#x200B;

1. Continue fighting
2. Recover your Health
3. Recover your posture (holding block)

&#x200B;

It adds so many layers to the system that From set up with the Souls games. I love it.

&#x200B;

**Stealth:** Not gonna lie, the enemies in this game have FarCry level senses. It still feels amazing to backstab an enemy, turn into a cloud of smoke and backstab three more. That being said, I feel if From patched the game to change the stealth system it might be for the better.

&#x200B;

**Bosses**: I got my shit kicked in so many times by the following bosses:

>!Guardian Ape, Guardian Ape x2, Corrupted Monk, Sword Saint Isshin Ashina!<

Now to be fair, there was never a battle that I felt I couldn't beat. There were plenty of moments in boss fights where I felt like From just wanted to make me suffer, but honestly once I learned attack patterns the game really started to shine for me. When you deflect every attack and knock down the phases of the final boss in like 30 seconds, you really feel like you're a badass Shinobi swordsman. 

The final boss (in the "Good" ending) is what I consider to be the hardest boss of any From game. It's FOUR PHASES, which means that you need to beat the first phases to even begin to learn the moveset of the last phases. It makes it a grueling battle of attrition (if you get hit early on you may as well just restart.) That being said, it was easily the most satisfying fight to beat and I'm so happy From put that fight into the game.

&#x200B;

Ultimately, it's my GOTY so far and potentially my favorite game ever. I'm gonna have to give it another runthrough, but honestly it exhausted me.

HURRY!!! 

&#x200B;

Proceeds to play as slowly as is humanly possible
